      "writtenOn": "2023-09-11T16:33:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For ease of testing, would it make sense to have a `getSupportedRoles` method that indicates whether a given IAuthGraphKeyExchange instance supports being a source, a sink, or both?  \n\nOr are we assuming that all instances will support either role (which shouldn\u0027t be too hard for vendors as long as they\u0027re based on the reference implementation)?",
      "revId": "e1b88a4e2ed36b3f4a294982359f7253c88b9776",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4e60cf80_27e166c7",
        "filename": "security/authgraph/aidl/android/hardware/security/authgraph/IAuthGraphKeyExchange.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 28
      },
      "lineNbr": 40,
      "author": {
        "id": 1678423
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-12T05:10:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`source`/ `sink` distinction actually matters in the context of the auth-bound key management feature of AuthgGraph.\n\nBut I added it here because the persistent arcs created during auth-bound key management stage are based on the channel arcs created in the key agreement stage.\n\nIn the key agreement protocol, this role distinction is used when a party is attaching their identity and the peer\u0027s identity in the protected header. When the key agreement methods are dedicated to either source or sink, they can attach the identity in the protected header using the proper keys (i.e. `source_identity` or `sink_identity`). If we are only concerned about the key agreement protocol, we can simply use `self_identity` and `peer_identity` for this purpose, but I thought it does not extend well when we want to build the auth-bound key management feature on top of the key agreement protocol.\n\nFor the use-cases that use only the key agreement protocol (e.g. LLVM), this distinction is not very relevant, IMO. I assumed that the party who orchestrates the key agreement would know who is a source and who is a sink (in case of GK and Keymint, if the lock setting service orchestrates the key agreement, we can assume that the lock setting service knows that GK is source and KM is sink). \n\nTherefore, I am not yet sure if we need a method like `getSupportedRoles`. But I will keep this question open for now and will try to get @paulcrowley@google.com\u0027s opinion too.",
      "parentUuid": "d5ba702e_c6ce2ba2",
      "revId": "e1b88a4e2ed36b3f4a294982359f7253c88b9776",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
